**(a) Pseudocode and flowchart (2 marks)**

- A **pseudocode** is a *text* description of an algorithm, written in structured, English-like statements (`read`, `write`, `while ... do`, `if ... then`) with indentation. It is compact, close to a programming language, and easy to edit and to convert into a program, but the flow of control is less visible.
- A **flowchart** is a *graphical* description of an algorithm, made of standard symbols (oval for start and stop, parallelogram for input and output, rectangle for a process, diamond for a decision) joined by arrows which show the flow of control. It shows the logic at a glance, but it takes more space and is harder to modify.

**(b) Lines 8 to 11 (1 mark)**

It is a **loop** (an iteration or repetition structure): a pre-test **WHILE loop** (`while (i <= N) do ... endwhile`), whose body is lines 9 and 10 and which is repeated as long as the condition $i \leq N$ is true.

**(c) Output of the algorithm (4 marks)**

Trace table for $N = 4$ ($F$ starts at 1 and $i$ at 1):

| i | $i \leq N$ ? | F after $F := F \times i$ | i after $i := i + 1$ |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | yes | 1 | 2 |
| 2 | yes | 2 | 3 |
