**(1) $N = 4$: the value printed is $F = 24$** (the pseudocode prints "the value of F is 24").

For $N = 6$ the loop runs for $i = 1$ to 6: $F = 1, 2, 6, 24, 120, 720$.

**(2) $N = 6$: the value printed is $F = 720$** (the pseudocode prints "the value of F is 720").

**(d) What the algorithm does (1 mark)**

It computes and prints the **factorial** of $N$: $F = N! = 1 \times 2 \times 3 \times \dots \times N$ (and $F = 1$ when $N \leq 1$).

**(e) Equivalent recursive algorithm (5 marks)**

The factorial is defined by $0! = 1! = 1$ and $n! = n \times (n-1)!$ for $n > 1$, so the loop is replaced by a function that calls itself:

```text
Algorithm WhatAmIDoingRecursive;
var N : integer;

function Fact(k : integer) : integer;
start
    if k <= 1 then
        Fact := 1                    { base case }
    else
        Fact := k * Fact(k - 1)      { recursive case }
    endif
end;

start
    write("enter an integer");
    read(N);
    write("the value of F is", Fact(N));
stop.
```

Execution for $N = 4$: $Fact(4) = 4 \times Fact(3) = 4 \times 3 \times Fact(2) = 4 \times 3 \times 2 \times Fact(1) = 4 \times 3 \times 2 \times 1 = 24$.

The same algorithm in ANSI C:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

/* Recursive factorial: fact(n) = n * fact(n - 1), with fact(0) = fact(1) = 1 */
long fact(int k)
{
    if (k <= 1)
        return 1L;                  /* base case */
    return k * fact(k - 1);         /* recursive case */
}

int main(void)
{
    int n;

    printf("enter an integer: ");
    scanf("%d", &n);
    printf("the value of F is %ld\n", fact(n));
    return 0;
}
```

and in ISO Pascal:

```pascal
program WhatAmIDoingRecursive;
var
  n : integer;

function Fact(k : integer) : longint;
begin
  if k <= 1 then
    Fact := 1
  else
    Fact := k * Fact(k - 1)
end;

begin
  write('enter an integer: ');
  readln(n);
  writeln('the value of F is ', Fact(n))
end.
```

